# Normalise line endings in the repository to LF.
#
# Without this, `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows means the working tree and the
# index disagree about every line of every text file. Git mostly hides that,
# but a merge sees two sides whose line endings were normalised at different
# times as having rewritten the whole file — so trivially compatible edits come
# back as full-file conflicts. Pinning LF in the repo makes the stored bytes
# identical no matter which machine or editor produced them.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must keep CRLF or cmd.exe/PowerShell mis-parse them.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts break outright with CRLF — the shebang keeps the \r.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary: never touch, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary

# SVG is text and worth diffing, but it is generated as often as hand-written.
*.svg text eol=lf

# Lockfiles are machine-written; a conflict in one is never resolved by hand.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
